From 35ef14d6778a52e449da10c408e2d3e62707beab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 18:49:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0320 -- decode a long WAV clip progressively,
 one chunk ahead of the mixer (opt-in)

WHY: a clip's first play decoded the whole clip at Open(), on the main
thread, inside one tick. In Trilby room 35 the end-of-dialogue ambient
(sound18.wav, 288 KB) costs 263 ms at DOSBox-X's fixed 40000, 211 ms of
it decode, which is most of the S8 fixture's 430 ms end-of-dialogue
stall. In 5 Days room 3, sound10.wav (215 KB) is the candidate for S16's
~395 ms stall (dosags docs/lab/s8.md). Chunk-ahead decoding shrinks the
stall; it does not move it.

WHY OPT-IN: it changes where decode time lands during play. On DOSBox-X
the worst slice is 5-6 ms per 2048 samples and up to 10 ms in one tick
(two slices) during a clip's first ~19 ticks. On the 486 a slice may
cost several times that inside a play tick against the 25 ms budget, so
the default flips only after a 486 same-binary ABBA (as 0239 did).

WHAT: with DOSAGS_PCM_INCR=1, a WAV PCM clip whose decode is one data
chunk (mono or stereo, 8/16-bit) and longer than two 2048-sample chunks,
played from bytes that stay alive (the sound cache's buffer), gets its
full-size device buffer at Open() and only the first chunk decoded. Each
GetData() decodes the chunk it is about to hand out. Output sample i
reads only source frames floor(i * rate / RATE) and the next, so a range
decode (dos_audio_decode_range: decode_wav's chunk walk,
decode_pcm_block's per-frame downmix and append_resampled_mono's
arithmetic, with i0/rem started from i * rate exactly) produces the
whole-clip decode's samples. The PCM cache (0216) keeps the progressive
object, so a later play continues it.
- Default (unset, or any value but 1): decodes whole, as before.
- DOSAGS_PCM_INCR=1 turns it on.
- DOSAGS_PCM_INCR_VERIFY=1 (with INCR=1) compares each completed buffer
  with the whole-clip decode.
- The pcmcache status line gains incr/incr_done/incr_slices/
  incr_verified/incr_mismatches (incr=0 in a default run).
